In the conversation, James asked about the relationship between the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC), \( R^2 \), and RMSE, particularly whether the AIC increases with \( R^2 \) or with decreasing RMSE. I explained that while there isn't a direct correlation, higher log-likelihood (associated with lower residual error) typically leads to lower AIC values. I clarified that \( R^2 \) and RMSE measure different aspects of model performance, and both can indirectly influence AIC through their relationship with model likelihood. James then inquired about how log-likelihood and residual error are related. I elaborated that minimizing residual error in regression models increases likelihood, thereby increasing log-likelihood, establishing a connection between these two concepts.
